Factory Pattern (Interview Answer):
-----------------------------------
Used to create objects without exposing creation logic.

Why Useful:
------------
1. Centralizes object creation.  
2. Hides complex "new" logic from client code.  
3. Easy to add new classes without breaking old code.  
4. Makes system scalable and maintainable.  

+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| Real Interview Example               | How Factory is Used                       | Why It’s Useful                         |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 1. Logger System                     | Creates FileLogger or ConsoleLogger.      | Switch log type easily.                |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 2. Payment Gateway                   | Returns Razorpay, Paytm, or Stripe obj.   | Add new gateway fast.                  |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 3. Notification Service              | Creates Email, SMS, or PushNotifier.      | Unified message handling.              |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 4. Database Connector                | Creates MySQL or MongoDB client.          | Change DB via config.                  |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 5. GUI Toolkit                       | Creates WindowsButton or MacButton.       | Works on any OS.                       |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 6. Vehicle Factory                   | Creates Car, Bike, or Auto object.        | Easy to extend types.                  |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
| 7. Cloud Provider SDK                | Returns AWS, Azure, or GCP client.        | Multi-cloud support.                   |
+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+



#include <iostream>        // for cout
#include <memory>          // for unique_ptr and make_unique
#include <string>          // for string
using namespace std;

class Button {             // Abstract base class (common interface for all buttons)
public:
    virtual void paint() = 0;   // Pure virtual function — every button must define this
    virtual ~Button() {}        // Virtual destructor for proper cleanup
};

// ---------- Concrete Products ----------
class WindowsButton : public Button {     // Concrete class for Windows-style button
public:
    void paint() override {               // Implementation of paint for Windows
        cout << "Painting Windows Button\n";  // Print message for demo
    }
};

class MacButton : public Button {         // Concrete class for Mac-style button
public:
    void paint() override {               // Implementation of paint for Mac
        cout << "Painting Mac Button\n";      // Print message for demo
    }
};

// ---------- Factory ----------
class ButtonFactory {                     // Factory class to create Button objects
public:
    static unique_ptr<Button> createButton(const string &osType) {  // Static factory method
        if (osType == "Windows")                                  // If OS is Windows
            return make_unique<WindowsButton>();                   // Create WindowsButton
        else if (osType == "Mac")                                 // If OS is Mac
            return make_unique<MacButton>();                       // Create MacButton
        else
            return nullptr;                                        // Invalid OS → return null
    }
};

int main() {
    auto button = ButtonFactory::createButton("Windows");   // Ask factory for Windows button
    if (button) button->paint();                            // Use the created button
    return 0;
}

